  "transcript": "# Dunkelflaute and the risks of electricity intermittency\n\n## What is Dunkelflaute and how risky is it?\n> Ministers accept Dunkelflaute means extended low wind and solar and is a genuine planning issue, but say there's no single agreed definition and the system already plans for such stresses.\n\nChair: You said the capacity market will meet demand during a Dunkelflaute. How are you defining a Dunkelflaute period?\nMichael Shanks: We characterise it as a period of low wind and low sun, often in winter with higher demand, but there isn't one universally accepted definition.\nChair: If the Met Office says three weeks at 20% wind is a one‑in‑40‑year event, can our system cope?\nMichael Shanks: The system is designed with redundancies to handle strains. NESO and operators plan for many weather and outage scenarios; Dunkelflaute is one of them.\nBill Jones: NESO already includes Dunkelflaute in its planning. It’s an ongoing challenge, becoming more relevant as renewables grow, but not new to the system.\n\n\n## The Strategic Spatial Energy Plan and timing\n> Government has six NESO pathways and will publish its chosen pathway and environmental assessments early next year, with a final decision by end of 2027.\n\nLord Krebs: Ofgem and NESO couldn't say which technologies the plan will favour. Can you explain the strategic spatial energy plan (SSEP) and timing?\nMichael Shanks: NESO gave us six pathways with costs and land‑use impacts. The Secretary of State will announce a preferred pathway in the first quarter of next year and a final decision by late 2027.\nLord Krebs: Will the plan favour proven technologies first, since it's updated every three years?\nMichael Shanks: The first plan will favour deployable, lower‑cost options to protect affordability, but won't shut out emerging tech; it's a broad design to guide grid investment.\nLord Krebs: When will investors get enough clarity to commit to new factories and supply chains?\nMichael Shanks: The plan gives line‑of‑sight: industry will see the chosen pathway early next year and the alternatives in the environmental appraisal, which supports investment decisions.\n\n\n## Capacity market, auctions and storage\n> The Capacity Market is technology‑neutral and prioritises availability; government is considering reforms to better value long and ultra‑long storage, but changes need evidence and time.\n\nBaroness Whitaker: Does the Capacity Market reform plan to reward ultra‑long‑duration storage and low‑carbon dispatchable power?\nBill Jones: The Capacity Market buys availability for security of supply; it's technology neutral. It wasn't meant to pick winners—other mechanisms bring new tech forward.\nBaroness Whitaker: So new tech needs separate support before joining the Capacity Market?\nBill Jones: Yes—CfDs, bespoke business models, R&D funding. We're actively thinking about reforms to value long‑duration characteristics, but changes need industry evidence. Proposals are planned next year.\nBaroness Rafferty: Are auctions the best way to secure capacity?\nMichael Shanks: Auctions give price competition and investor certainty. We run auctions four years and one year ahead; rules are kept under review to reflect new technologies.\nChair: The Renewables Investors Group says storage longer than 9.5 hours gets little extra credit. Will you change that?\nBill Jones: We are reviewing de‑rating factors and valuation of longer duration. But ultra‑long technologies aren't yet operating at scale, so it's premature to finalise changes now.\nMichael Shanks: Ofgem's cap and floor has delivered ~7GW of long‑duration projects; there will be a second window. UKRI has £28m for ultra‑long‑duration storage R&D to help build evidence.\n\n\n## Gas, hydrogen, biomethane and CCUS: transition and costs\n> Gas will remain a strategic, dispatchable back‑up for years. Government is exploring options—refurbishment, CCUS, strategic reserve—and will publish further gas‑security work next year.\n\nLord Rooker: Ofgem/NESO say unabated gas will back us until low‑carbon alternatives arrive—do you have a plan to displace unabated gas, and by when?\nMichael Shanks: Gas remains important for decades. We want to reduce its role, add CCUS where feasible, expand storage and other dispatchable options—but we can't give a one‑line switch‑off date.\nLord Jay of Ewelme: What about the cost of extending gas plants' lives and the effect on bills compared with alternatives?\nMichael Shanks: Refurbishment can be cheaper than new build, but gas plants also have capital costs. Overall, systems with more renewables, nuclear and storage are cheaper long term than a gas‑dependent system.\nBill Jones: Gas capacity sits at ~35GW but runs fewer hours now; that reduces revenue and complicates investability. The Capacity Market helps, but decisions on new build versus refurb are complex.\nLord Ashcombe: What about hydrogen storage, CCUS, and using excess wind to make hydrogen?\nMichael Shanks: We back hydrogen and CCUS pilots like Net Zero Teesside. The hydrogen strategy is imminent; we're pragmatic about realistic deployment rather than headline targets. Offshore storage is likely.\nBaroness Rafferty: And biomethane—will it scale?\nMichael Shanks: Biomethane helps use waste and injects into the gas grid, but it's finite and small scale. Green Gas Support will add plants but expectations should be realistic.\nBaroness Coffey: The UK has very little gas storage compared with EU states. Will you commit to a strategic storage decision soon?\nMichael Shanks: We issued an interim response and will publish a fuller assessment; strategic storage is an option but costs billions and we must weigh resilience versus price for consumers.\n\n\n## The transmission grid and investment trade‑offs\n> Ministers say decades of underinvestment left the grid constrained; connections reform cleared queues but major network upgrades, like Sea Link, are urgent and costly, with trade‑offs for bills.\n\nLord Redwood: You hinted at an excess of wind in Scotland and grid blockage. How will you unblock connections and what will it cost?\nMichael Shanks: The grid is resilient but underinvested. We've reformed connections, clearing 300GW of a 700GW queue, and must upgrade transmission—it's expensive but cheaper than curtailing renewables and running gas.\nLord Ashcombe: How long before major links and synchronous equipment arrive so spilled wind can be used?\nMichael Shanks: Projects like Sea Link have got DCO approval now. Building major transmission takes years; we must invest now to avoid even greater costs later from constraints.\nEarl Russell: How will government help the UK build supply chains so turbines and turbines' parts aren't delayed by global demand?\nMichael Shanks: We're mapping supply chains and seeking to onshore more manufacturing to reduce reliance on global bottlenecks and improve security of delivery.\nChair: To finish, do you accept the scale of the challenge and will you give clarity next year?\nMichael Shanks: Yes. The challenge is large but manageable. We'll publish the SSEP pathway next year, a hydrogen strategy soon, and further gas‑security work in due course.\nChair: Thank you both for clear, candid answers. That concludes our public session.\n",
